grid-windowing.ts:38-42:
export function computeColumns(containerWidth: number, itemWidth: number, gap: number): number {
  if (containerWidth <= 0 || itemWidth <= 0) return 1;
  const columns = Math.floor((containerWidth + gap) / (itemWidth + gap));
  return Math.max(1, columns);
}

Cells are placed at VirtualPosterGrid.tsx:271 `const left = col * (actualItemWidth + gap);` and stamped inline at :278 `style={{ transform: \`translate(${left}px, ${top}px)\`, width: actualItemWidth }}`.

CONSEQUENCE FOR LD-14: at a 380px viewport containerWidth = 348px, so computeColumns(348, 168, 16) = floor(364/184) = 1 -> ONE 348px-wide jumbo poster. The `.skeletonGrid` CSS produces the same 1 column. Implementing "repeat(2, 1fr) below ~480px" therefore requires BOTH a CSS change (.skeletonGrid) AND a JS change (computeColumns / DEFAULT_ITEM_WIDTH override or a breakpoint-aware column clamp in VirtualPosterGrid.tsx). A CSS-only edit will change the skeleton only and leave the real grid at 1 jumbo column.

